    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#george-v"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-v.jpg" alt="George V" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#george-v">George V</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_George_1923_LCCN2014715558_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bassano Ltd, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The present United Kingdom took shape with the partition of Ireland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a>, created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1800, had faced a war of independence in Ireland from 1919 to 1921. The Government of Ireland Act of 1920 set up separate home-rule parliaments for Northern and Southern Ireland, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> of December 1921 provided for an Irish Free State with dominion status. When the Free State came into being on 6 December 1922, the parliament of Northern Ireland exercised its right to opt out the next day, leaving the six north-eastern counties within the United Kingdom. The remaining state was otherwise continuous with its predecessor: the same Crown, Parliament at Westminster, civil service, armed forces and laws. The Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act of 1927 changed the formal name to the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> and Northern Ireland. In 1922 the state of about 44 million people, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-v">George V</a> and the Conservative Prime Minister Andrew Bonar Law, still ruled the largest empire in history, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#palestine">Palestine</a>, Transjordan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>, possessed the world's largest navy alongside that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> and was a leading member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, though it was heavily indebted after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between the wars the old staple industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a>, shipbuilding and iron declined, leaving mass unemployment in South Wales, the North of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, Clydeside and Northern Ireland; the General Strike of May 1926 and the Jarrow March of 1936 became symbols of the era. Newer industries such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals grew in the Midlands and the South East, and suburban home ownership spread. Women gained the vote on equal terms with men in 1928. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a> brought total mobilisation, rationing and the Beveridge Report of 1942, which inspired the post-war welfare state: the Labour government of Clement Attlee nationalised coal, railways and other industries and founded the National Health Service in 1948. Immigration from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a>, symbolised by the arrival of the Empire Windrush in 1948, and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-asia">South Asia</a> and elsewhere, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> an increasingly multi-ethnic society. The affluence of the 1950s and 1960s was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a> and industrial conflict in the 1970s. From 1979 the governments of Margaret Thatcher privatised state industries, curbed trade unions and deregulated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">City of London</a>, accelerating a shift from manufacturing to services and finance. Religious practice declined steadily, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#church-of-england">Church of England</a> remains established, and today about 68 million people live in the UK.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> accepted parity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> under the Washington Naval Treaty, and the Royal Air Force, created in 1918, policed parts of the empire from the air. Rearmament from the mid-1930s produced the Hurricane and Spitfire fighters and the Chain Home radar network. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a> the United Kingdom declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> on 3 September 1939, evacuated over 330,000 Allied troops from Dunkirk in 1940, held off the Luftwaffe in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, fought on until joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the United States in 1941. British and Commonwealth forces won at El Alamein in 1942, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burma">Burma</a>, and landed in Normandy on D-Day, 6 June 1944. About 450,000 British servicemen and civilians died. After 1945 the UK was a founding member of NATO, tested its first atomic bomb in 1952 and has kept a submarine-based nuclear deterrent since the late 1960s. Its forces fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a>, in colonial conflicts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malaya">Malaya</a>, Kenya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyprus">Cyprus</a>, at Suez in 1956 and in the Falklands War of 1982, and were deployed in Northern Ireland from 1969 to 2007. Later operations included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gulf-war">Gulf War</a> of 1991, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 2001 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> from 2003.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#elizabeth-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii.jpg" alt="Elizabeth II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Elizabeth_II_official_portrait_for_1959_tour_(retouched)_(cropped)_(3-to-4_aspect_ratio).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donald McKague, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> without a single written constitution; its constitution rests on statute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#common-law">common law</a> and convention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> lies with the Crown in Parliament, and executive power is exercised by a Prime Minister and Cabinet answerable to the elected House of Commons, chosen by first-past-the-post voting. The power of the unelected House of Lords was limited by the Parliament Acts of 1911 and 1949, and most hereditary peers were removed in 1999. The monarchs of the period have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-v">George V</a>, Edward VIII, who abdicated in 1936, George VI, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>, the longest-reigning British monarch from 1952 to 2022, and Charles III. Three legal systems coexist: English law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> and Wales, Scots law and Northern Irish law. Northern Ireland had its own parliament at Stormont until 1972, when it was suspended amid the Troubles and direct rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a> was imposed. Devolution in 1999 created a Scottish Parliament, a National Assembly for Wales and, under the Good Friday Agreement of 1998, a power-sharing Northern Ireland Assembly. The Human Rights Act of 1998 and the creation of a Supreme Court in 2009 added new judicial checks. A large, permanent, politically neutral civil service, shaped by the Northcote–Trevelyan reforms of the nineteenth century, administers government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#united-nations-security-council"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council.jpg" alt="United Nations Security Council" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_United_Nations.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joowwww, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom's influence in the twentieth century was immense. Its resistance to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> in 1940–1941, when it was the only major power still fighting, was decisive for the outcome of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the codebreakers of Bletchley Park, among them Alan Turing, contributed to Allied victory and to the birth of computing. As a permanent member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a>, a founder of NATO and a nuclear power, it shaped the post-war order, and John Maynard Keynes helped design the Bretton Woods system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#decolonisation">Decolonisation</a> turned most of the empire into independent states, many of which joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a> and kept parliamentary institutions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#westminster-system">Westminster model</a>, English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#common-law">common law</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#english-language">English language</a>. British science produced penicillin, the jet engine, the structure of DNA, discovered at Cambridge in 1953, and the first cloned mammal, Dolly the sheep. British popular culture, from the BBC, founded in 1922, to the Beatles, James Bond and the Premier League, reached a global audience, and English became the world's chief language of international communication, a position reinforced by both British and American influence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a> remained one of the world's leading financial centres.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#suez-crisis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-crisis.jpg" alt="Suez Crisis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Said_from_air.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fleet Air Arm official photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The United Kingdom still exists, but its place in the world has been transformed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a> left it victorious but nearly bankrupt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>'s independence in 1947 began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#decolonisation">decolonisation</a> that accelerated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 exposed the limits of British power; most African and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> colonies became independent in the 1960s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#handover-of-hong-kong">handover of Hong Kong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> in 1997 ended the empire as a major territorial power. Economic growth lagged behind that of Western European rivals, the pound was devalued in 1949 and 1967, and the government sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> in 1976. Having declined to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> at its foundation, the UK entered it in 1973 under Edward Heath and confirmed membership by referendum in 1975. The Good Friday Agreement of 1998 largely ended a conflict in Northern Ireland that had cost about 3,500 lives, and Scots voted to remain in the union in 2014. In a referendum in 2016, 51.9 per cent voted to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#european-union">European Union</a>, and the UK left on 31 January 2020. Today it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under Charles III, one of the world's largest economies, a nuclear power and a permanent member of the Security Council.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The United Kingdom, in its post-1922 form, led the last phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#british-empire">British Empire</a>, stood against Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> in 1940, founded a welfare state and shaped the post-war international order; its passage from imperial power to a European and then post-European middle power is a central story of twentieth-century history.</em></p>

